# Semantic Versioning Configuration for llm-evals

## Commit Convention

Every commit message MUST follow the conventional commit format:

```
<type>(<scope>): <description>
```

Supported types:
| Type     | Usage                                     |
|----------|-------------------------------------------|
| feat     | New user-facing feature                   |
| fix      | Bug fix                                   |
| docs     | Documentation changes                     |
| test     | Adding or fixing tests                    |
| refact   | Code refactoring (no behavior change)     |
| refactor | Alias for refact                          |
| perf     | Performance improvement                   |
| chore    | Tooling, dependencies, version bumps      |
| ci       | CI/CD configuration                       |
| build    | Build system changes                      |
| deps     | Dependency updates                        |
| style    | Formatting only                           |
| revert   | Reverting a previous commit               |

A `BREAKING:` prefix at the start marks the commit as a major version bump.

## Semver Bump Rules (applied by release.py)

| Commit type  | Bump    | Notes                                    |
|--------------|---------|------------------------------------------|
| feat         | minor   | New user-visible functionality           |
| fix          | patch   | Bug fixes                                |
| fix+BREAKING | major   | Fix that breaks backward compat          |
| test         | patch   | Test-only changes                        |
| refact       | patch   | Refactoring (no behavior change)         |
| docs         | patch   | Documentation                            |
| chore        | patch   | Tooling, housekeeping                    |
| BREAKING:    | major   | Always — breaks existing contracts       |

Rule: **highest wins**. A mix of feat + fix → minor. A single BREAKING → major.

## Release Workflow

### Manual release (recommended — use release.py)

```bash
# Auto-detect bump type from conventional commits since last tag:
uv run python release.py

# Force a specific bump:
uv run python release.py patch
uv run python release.py minor
uv run python release.py major

# Preview without making changes:
uv run python release.py --dry-run
```

This script:
1. Scans commits since last tag for conventional commit types
2. Derives the correct semver bump
3. Updates `version` in `pyproject.toml`
4. Generates a new CHANGELOG.md section from git history
5. Pre-pends to CHANGELOG.md (newest at top)
6. Commits the version bump
7. Creates an annotated git tag (`v1.2.3`)
8. Prints the push command you need to run

### CI/CD auto-release (GitHub Actions)

Pushing to `main` automatically triggers `.github/workflows/release.yml`, which:
1. Detects the next patch version
2. Bumps pyproject.toml
3. Re-generates CHANGELOG.md
4. Commits and tags

The CI skips auto-release on "chore: bump version" commits to avoid loops.

### First release

```bash
# After merging your first batch of commits to main:
uv run python release.py minor    # or patch, depending on changes
git push origin main --tags
```

## Commit message validation

A git commit-msg hook is provided at `scripts/commit-msg-hook`.
Install it once:
```bash
bash scripts/install-hooks.sh
```

This validates every commit message against the conventional format before
allowing the commit. CI also validates via the `.commitlintrc.json` rules.

## Quick reference

```bash
# Write code, commit with conventional messages:
git commit -m "feat: add arena comparison view"
git commit -m "fix: handle empty dataset gracefully"
git commit -m "BREAKING: feat: redesign eval API contract"

# When ready to release:
uv run python release.py           # auto-detect + tag
git push origin main --tags        # push release to GitHub

# View changelog for current unreleased changes:
uv run python changelog_generator.py
```